ght pair with T5/T7 for chow, but T5 and T7 are available (T5:1, T7:2). Actually, T6 could form chow with T5 and T7, but you only have one T6. So keeping T6 doesn't help much unless you draw T5 or T7. Similarly, B2 doesn't help unless you draw B1/B3/B4. Both are equal.  \n\nBut note: Your hand has many pairs; you might be close to a \"All Pungs\" hand (all melds are pungs/kongs). You already have two pungs (B7, F2). If you can get two more pungs from W1, W2, or B5, that would be a good hand (All Pungs pattern worth 6 points). So you should keep the pairs and discard the lone tiles. Among B2 and T6, which is more dangerous? Looking at discards: B2 has been discarded by across player? In history: across player \"Chi B2\" means they made a chow using B2 (so they have B2 in a meld). That means B2 is less likely to be needed by others. T6: you played T6 earlier, and next player played B6, across played T4, previous played T5. T6 might be somewhat safe. But I think B2 is slightly safer because it's already used in a chow by across.  \n\nAlso, consider scoring: All Pungs would give 6 points, plus you have F2 pung which might match Seat Wind? Your Seat Wind is West Wind (F3), not F2. Prevailing Wind is North (F4). So no extra points there. But you could also have Pung of Terminals or Honors for F2 (1 point each). And if you get a pung of W1 or W2 (terminals), that's another point. Plus maybe All Simples (no terminals/honors) but you have F2 and W1/W2 are terminals, so not All Simples. So you need at least 8 points total; All Pungs (6) + maybe 2 from Pung of Terminals/Honors (F2 and a terminal pung) = 8 points. That could work. So aim for pungs.  \n\nThus, discard a lone tile. Between B2 and T6, I choose B2 as it seems safer.  \n\nReason: My hand has two pungs already and three pairs (W1, W2, B5). I aim to complete a hand with All Pungs pattern (6 points) plus additional points from pung of honors/terminals to reach 8 points. Discarding the isolated B2 is safe because an opponent has already used B2 in a chow, and it doesn't break any potential melds in my hand. Keeping the pairs maintains flexibility to pung W1, W2, or B5.  \n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
