cAll Pungs\u201d or \u201cDragon Pung\u201d-like patterns with winds).  \n\n4. **Defensive consideration:**  \n   - Previous player has melds of W5 and B2 \u2014 he might be collecting W1\u2013W9 sequences or low numbers. W1/W2 might be dangerous.  \n   - Next player has W8 pung \u2014 may be collecting W8\u2013W9 related. W9 might be safer.  \n\nBest discard candidates:  \n- **W9** is a terminal, discarding helps \u201cAll Simples\u201d, relatively safe.  \n- **F2** is honor, discarding also helps \u201cAll Simples\u201d and reduces honor reliance, but might be riskier.  \n- **T6** is simple but isolated, less useful for sequences.  \n\n**Final choice:** Discard W9 because:  \n- It\u2019s a terminal, removing it moves toward \u201cAll Simples\u201d.  \n- It appears safe given both next and across players discarded W9 earlier.  \n- It doesn\u2019t break any promising meld in my hand (W1,W2 still can form chow with W3).  \n\n**Reason:** Choose W9 for safety and to aim for \u201cAll Simples\u201d while keeping useful pairs (W1,W2,B5) and pung-ready F2.  \n\n**Answer:** Play W9"}
