I have two W2, unshown W2=4, no recent discards of W2 (previous played T2, not W2). Might be less safe.  \n\nBest discard likely B5 or B4, as they are recent discards by previous player and seem safe, and they don't break promising combinations. B5 is more redundant since I have two and no B6. B4 is single. Discarding B5 keeps B4 for possible chow if I draw B3 (unshown B3=1) or B6 (0), but B6 gone, so B4-B5 chow not possible without B6. So B5 is expendable.  \n\nAlso consider potential fan: If I keep F2 pung and B7 pung, and aim for all pungs hand (6 fan) plus maybe dragon pung (none), wind pung (F2 not prevailing/seat), etc. Need more pungs. So need to draw pairs to pung. Discarding singles like T6, B4 might be better. But B5 is a pair, discarding one breaks pair, but I still have one B5 left which is not useful alone.  \n\nActually, having two B5 is a pair, which could be the final pair if I get three other pungs. So breaking B5 pair might not be good. Better to keep pairs. So discard singles: T6 or B4. Between them, B4 is safer based on recent discards. T6 might be riskier.  \n\nThus, discard B4.  \n\nReason: B4 is a single tile with no strong combination potential (B4-B5 chow impossible due to no B6 left, B3-B4 chow possible if draw B3, but unshown B3=1, low chance). It has been recently discarded by previous player, making it relatively safe. Keeping pairs (W1, W2, B5, F2) maintains flexibility for forming pungs or the final pair. Discarding B4 reduces hand clutter while minimizing risk.  \n\nAnswer: Play B4"}
